---
import BaseLayout from "@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import { buttonVariants } from "@/components/ui/button";
import heroImage from '../../assets/services/storage.jpg';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="What to Do If You Need Temporary Storage During a Move in Denver Colorado | Legacy Moving"
  description="Need short-term storage during your Denver move? Here's what your options actually are—storage solutions we can connect you with, storing items in the truck, and how to decide what works best."
>
  <!-- Hero Section -->
  <section class="relative bg-gradient-to-r from-secondary to-secondary/90 text-white py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-20">
      <Image src={heroImage} alt="Temporary storage solutions during a move in Denver Colorado" class="w-full h-full object-cover" />
    </div>
    <div class="container relative z-10">
      <div class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="text-white/80 mb-3">April 16, 2026 • 13 min read</p>
        <h1 class="font-display font-bold text-3xl md:text-5xl mb-4">
          What Should You Do If You Need Temporary Storage During a Move in Denver Colorado
        </h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-white/90">
          The gap between your move-out date and move-in date doesn't have to be a crisis. Here's what your real options are, and how we can help bridge the gap.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Article Content -->
  <article class="py-12 md:py-16">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto prose prose-lg">

        <p class="lead">
          Here's a situation that comes up constantly in Denver real estate: you've sold your home and you need to be out by the 30th, but your new place doesn't close until the 10th of the following month. Or your apartment lease ends two weeks before your new rental becomes available. Or the house you're moving into needs some work done first, and you can't actually get your furniture in there until the contractor finishes.
        </p>

        <p>
          A gap between move-out and move-in is one of the most common—and most stressful—scenarios in any move. The question is: what do you do with all your stuff in the meantime? Where do your couch, your dining table, your boxes of kitchen equipment, and everything else actually go for those days or weeks in between?
        </p>

        <p>
          The good news is there are more options than most people realize. And depending on how long your gap is, some options are dramatically more practical than others. Let's break it down.
        </p>

        <h2>First: How Long Are We Actually Talking?</h2>

        <p>
          The length of your storage gap determines almost everything about which solution makes the most sense. The conversation splits roughly into three categories:
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A day or two.</strong> You're out of your old place Friday evening, moving into the new place Sunday morning. You need somewhere for your belongings to live for one overnight, maybe two. This is a very short-term bridge, and there are specific options that are a perfect fit for this.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>One to three weeks.</strong> A common real estate timing gap. Your belongings need somewhere safe for a week or two while the lease, closing, or renovation situation resolves itself. Short-term storage or a temporary arrangement makes sense here.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>A month or more.</strong> You're staging a home for sale and want furniture out of the way, or you're relocating to Denver from out of state and your new home isn't ready yet. This is a genuine longer-term storage situation that needs a real solution—whether that's a climate-controlled unit, a portable storage container, or some other arrangement.
        </p>

        <p>
          Each scenario has different best answers, so let's work through them.
        </p>

        <h2>Option 1: We Store It in the Truck (Super Short-Term Overnight Situations)</h2>

        <p>
          If you're dealing with a very short gap—overnight, or a day or two—one of the most practical solutions is simply leaving your belongings in the moving truck overnight. This is exactly what we do in certain situations, and it works well when the timing gap is short and the circumstances are right.
        </p>

        <p>
          What this looks like in practice: we load your belongings from your old place, the truck is secured overnight (we take security seriously—the truck is locked and not parked somewhere exposed), and we deliver to your new home the next morning as soon as you have access.
        </p>

        <p>
          This approach keeps your move as a single continuous job rather than two separate events. There's no unloading and reloading from a storage unit, which means less handling of your items, less time, and typically less cost. For a short gap, it's often the cleanest option available.
        </p>

        <p>
          It does have limits. We wouldn't recommend storing temperature-sensitive items in a truck through an overnight if there's going to be extreme weather. Denver nights in winter can drop well below freezing, and Denver afternoons in summer can make the interior of a truck extremely hot. Things like houseplants, electronics, musical instruments, and temperature-sensitive artwork aren't great candidates for overnight truck storage. But for most household goods and furniture, it's a completely viable solution.
        </p>

        <p>
          If this is a route you're considering, just bring it up when you're booking your move or talking to our team. It's a normal thing to arrange, not an unusual request. <a href="/contact">Contact us</a> to talk through the specifics of your timeline and we'll tell you honestly whether truck storage makes sense for your situation.
        </p>

        <h2>Option 2: Short-Term Storage Solutions We Can Help You Coordinate</h2>

        <p>
          For gaps that are more than a couple of days, a storage unit is usually the right answer—and this is where we can be genuinely helpful even though we don't own storage facilities ourselves.
        </p>

        <p>
          We have established relationships with reputable storage providers in the Denver metro area, and we can connect you with the right option based on your specific needs: how much you need to store, how long, what kind of access you need, whether climate control matters, and where you're located. This takes a lot of the legwork out of finding a decent storage unit on your own when you're already dealing with the hundred other things a move involves.
        </p>

        <p>
          What we actually do: we can pick up from your old home, bring your items to the storage facility, and then make a second trip to pull everything from storage and deliver it to your new home when you're ready. One moving company handling both legs of the move means better continuity, better handling of your things, and one point of contact rather than three.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our <a href="/services/storage-solutions">storage solutions page</a> has more detail on how we approach this and what we can help coordinate. For the short-term version specifically, check out our <a href="/short-term-storage">short-term storage information</a>—it's designed exactly for the kind of temporary gap situations we're describing here.
        </p>

        <h2>What Types of Storage Actually Make Sense in Denver?</h2>

        <p>
          Denver has a lot of storage options. Here's a quick breakdown of the main types and when each one is most appropriate:
        </p>

        <h3>Traditional Self-Storage Units</h3>

        <p>
          Self-storage facilities are the most common solution for temporary move storage. You rent a unit by the month (or sometimes by the week), load your items in, and access them whenever you need. Denver and its suburbs have no shortage of these—you'll find options in virtually every part of the metro from <a href="/cities/aurora">Aurora</a> to <a href="/cities/lakewood">Lakewood</a> to <a href="/cities/westminster">Westminster</a>.
        </p>

        <p>
          Key considerations: climate-controlled vs. non-climate-controlled. Denver has dry, high-altitude air that can actually be relatively kind to stored items—wood furniture doesn't warp the same way it might in a humid climate—but temperature extremes matter. In summer, a non-climate-controlled unit can get very hot, and electronics, instruments, artwork, candles, and anything with adhesive (photographs, certain furniture finishes) should ideally be in a climate-controlled space. In winter, extreme cold can damage certain items as well.
        </p>

        <p>
          For most standard household goods over a period of a few weeks, a standard non-climate-controlled unit is fine. For anything you'd consider valuable or sensitive, spend the extra few dollars per month on climate control.
        </p>

        <p>
          Unit sizing: a 10x10 unit (roughly the size of a standard bedroom) holds the contents of a one-bedroom apartment fairly comfortably. A 10x20 is a more appropriate starting point for a two or three bedroom home. When in doubt, err on the side of a slightly larger unit—running out of space and having to rent a second unit mid-move is expensive and inconvenient.
        </p>

        <h3>Portable Storage Containers</h3>

        <p>
          Companies like PODS and U-Pack deliver a storage container to your driveway, you load it at your own pace, and then they either store it at their facility or move it to your new address. This is a solid middle-ground option for people who want the flexibility of loading and unloading on their own schedule but still need the items to move from one place to another.
        </p>

        <p>
          In Denver, the practical consideration is driveway and street access. Older neighborhoods with limited driveway space or HOA restrictions may not be ideal for portable containers. It's worth checking ahead.
        </p>

        <p>
          Portable containers are often a good fit for longer-term storage gaps or for situations where you want to do a lot of the loading yourself. For most shorter-term gaps where you're already hiring movers, a traditional storage unit accessed through a moving company tends to be the cleaner solution.
        </p>

        <h3>Full-Service Storage Options</h3>

        <p>
          Full-service storage is a category that's grown significantly—companies where you request pickup, they come get your items, store them in their warehouse, and then deliver them back when you need them. You never actually visit a facility. This works well for people who want zero involvement in the storage logistics and are willing to pay for that convenience.
        </p>

        <p>
          The flip side is cost (typically higher per square foot than self-storage) and access (harder to get specific items out in a hurry if you realize you need something). For a temporary move storage situation where you need all your items at roughly the same time anyway, this can actually work quite well.
        </p>

        <p>
          The network of storage providers we work with includes options in this category. If you want to talk through whether this makes sense for your specific move, reach out through our <a href="/contact">contact page</a> or give us a call.
        </p>

        <h2>Planning Your Storage Move: Practical Steps</h2>

        <p>
          If you've determined that storage is the right solution for your situation, here's what the planning process looks like:
        </p>

        <h3>Know Your Timeline Before You Book Anything</h3>

        <p>
          Moving storage timing often shifts. Closings get delayed. Contractors run late. Lease start dates get pushed. Before you commit to a storage rental or a specific plan, get as clear as you can on the actual dates involved—move-out date, expected move-in date, and ideally what happens if the move-in date shifts a week in either direction.
        </p>

        <p>
          Storage facilities generally give you more flexibility if you book month-to-month rather than signing a longer commitment. For a temporary move gap, month-to-month is almost always the right structure.
        </p>

        <h3>Inventory What Needs to Be Stored</h3>

        <p>
          Not everything necessarily needs to go into storage. Items you'll need immediate access to during the gap—a few changes of clothes, toiletries, laptop, important documents, bedding if you're staying somewhere temporarily—should stay with you or in a suitcase. What goes into storage is everything else.
        </p>

        <p>
          Think about what you'll need access to during the storage period. If there's a chance you'll need to grab something specific from storage during the gap, keep that in mind when you're loading—don't bury it at the back of the unit under everything else.
        </p>

        <h3>Label Everything for Storage</h3>

        <p>
          Moving boxes that go into storage should be labeled as thoroughly as boxes going directly to a new home, but with one additional layer of information: priority or urgency. If you know you'll need a specific box within the first day or two of accessing storage, label it clearly and load it last (so it's first out of the unit).
        </p>

        <h3>Photograph Your Items Before Storage</h3>

        <p>
          Before anything goes into a storage unit, take quick photos of larger items and any items you'd consider valuable or difficult to replace. This is basic documentation that's useful if there's ever a question about the condition of items before and after storage. Most of the time you'll never look at these photos again—but you'll be glad to have them if something comes up.
        </p>

        <h2>Storage During Specific Denver Move Scenarios</h2>

        <h3>You're Selling and Staging Your Home</h3>

        <p>
          Denver's real estate market is competitive, and staged homes genuinely sell faster and at higher prices than cluttered, lived-in homes. A lot of homeowners in <a href="/cities/denver">Denver</a>, <a href="/cities/highlands-ranch">Highlands Ranch</a>, <a href="/cities/centennial">Centennial</a>, and across the metro use storage as part of a home staging strategy—move out about half the furniture and personal items, put them in a unit, and let the home show at its best.
        </p>

        <p>
          This is actually a situation where our approach works well: we can help you move a targeted set of items to a storage unit while you continue living in (and showing) the home, and then coordinate the final move when you have a buyer and a closing date.
        </p>

        <h3>You're Moving to Denver from Out of State</h3>

        <p>
          If you're relocating to Denver from another state and your new home won't be ready by the time your <a href="/services/long-distance-moving">long distance move</a> arrives, storage is almost a given. The timing coordination between a cross-country move and a real estate closing is notoriously tricky.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're arriving in Denver before your home is ready, it's worth thinking through where you'll be staying during the gap, whether you'll need any items from storage during that time (furniture for a furnished rental vs. an unfurnished one, for example), and how the storage-to-home delivery will be coordinated at the end. We've handled this scenario many times for people moving <a href="/long-distance-moving-services">into Denver from other states</a> and can help you think through the logistics.
        </p>

        <h3>You're In Between Leases</h3>

        <p>
          Lease gaps are extremely common in Denver's rental market, especially in competitive neighborhoods where you can't always line up your new lease start with your old lease end. If you're in this situation and you've got a few weeks between leases, storage for the bulk of your items combined with staying with friends, family, or a short-term rental situation is a workable plan.
        </p>

        <p>
          For people in <a href="/cities/denver">Denver</a>, <a href="/cities/aurora">Aurora</a>, <a href="/cities/thornton">Thornton</a>, <a href="/cities/englewood">Englewood</a>, or anywhere else in the metro area, we can move your items into storage at the end of your lease and then back out into your new place when it's ready—treating it as one coordinated move rather than two completely separate events.
        </p>

        <h3>Your New Home Needs Work Before You Can Move In</h3>

        <p>
          Bought a fixer-upper in Denver? Renovating the kitchen or bathrooms before you move in? This is another extremely common reason for a storage gap. In this case, you often have the keys to the new place but genuinely can't bring furniture and boxes in until the work is done.
        </p>

        <p>
          Storage bridges that gap. The added benefit here is that storing your items while contractors work means your belongings aren't getting covered in drywall dust or risking damage from tools and construction activity. It also means contractors can work more efficiently without your furniture in the way.
        </p>

        <p>
          Our <a href="/storage-services">storage services</a> are set up for exactly this kind of situation. When the renovation wraps up, we coordinate delivery of your stored items to the new home.
        </p>

        <h2>How Much Does Temporary Storage Cost in Denver?</h2>

        <p>
          Storage costs in Denver vary based on unit size, location, climate control, and facility quality, but here's a general sense of current market rates:
        </p>

        <p>
          A small 5x5 or 5x10 unit (for partial-home storage, overflow, or staging) typically runs in the range of $50 to $100 per month. A standard 10x10 unit (one bedroom apartment equivalent) usually falls between $100 and $175 per month. A 10x20 or larger (for two to three bedroom home storage) can range from $175 to $300+ per month, depending on climate control and location.
        </p>

        <p>
          Most facilities offer the first month discounted or free as a promotional offer—worth factoring in if you only need storage for a month or so. Beyond the unit cost, factor in the cost of the move-in and move-out trips. That's where having a single moving company coordinate the whole thing—pick up from old home, drop at storage, later deliver to new home—can actually save you money compared to coordinating multiple separate services.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you want an honest picture of what your full storage scenario would cost based on your specific situation, <a href="/get-quote">request a quote</a> and we can walk through the options with you.
        </p>

        <h2>What We Actually Recommend</h2>

        <p>
          If you're dealing with a day or two gap: truck storage. It's simple, keeps things together, and avoids the cost and hassle of a storage unit for a very short period.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're dealing with one to three weeks: a storage unit that we help you coordinate, with us handling both legs of the move. You get a reputable facility we've worked with, proper handling of your items at both ends, and one company to deal with from start to finish.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're dealing with a month or more: still a storage unit, but the decision between a traditional self-storage unit, portable container, or full-service storage depends on your specific situation. We can help you think through what makes the most sense based on where you are, what you need to store, and what your access needs look like during the storage period.
        </p>

        <p>
          The common thread across all of these is that storage situations are much less stressful when they're planned ahead rather than figured out the day before move-out. If you know there's going to be a gap in your move timeline, bring it up early—when you're getting your initial quote, or as soon as you find out the dates don't line up. That gives us time to coordinate the right solution rather than scrambling.
        </p>

        <h2>Quick Tips for Getting Through the Storage Gap with Less Stress</h2>

        <p>
          <strong>Pack a go-bag early.</strong> The items you'll need during the storage gap—clothes, toiletries, chargers, important documents, basic kitchen gear if you're staying somewhere temporarily—should be set aside well before move-out day and kept completely separate from everything going into storage. Don't let this stuff get mixed in with the main move.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Keep your most important documents with you.</strong> Passports, social security cards, birth certificates, lease agreements, closing documents—none of these should go into storage. They stay with you, period.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Load storage strategically.</strong> Items you might need to access during the gap go in last (closest to the door). Items you definitely won't need until delivery go in first (deepest in the unit). A few minutes of planning at load-in makes retrieval much easier.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Give yourself buffer time on both ends.</strong> When planning your storage window, add a few extra days on the back end in case your move-in date shifts. Running out of time on your storage rental and scrambling to extend it in the middle of a move is avoidable stress.
        </p>

        <p>
          <strong>Check your renters or homeowners insurance.</strong> Your existing renters or homeowners insurance policy may cover items in storage—worth checking before you decide whether to add storage-specific insurance through the facility. Many self-storage facilities offer it, but it's not always necessary if you already have coverage.
        </p>

        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

        <p>
          A storage gap during a Denver move is a common, solvable problem. The key is not leaving it to the last minute. Whether the solution is leaving your belongings in our truck overnight, connecting you with a storage facility we know and trust, or coordinating a multi-step move-in and move-out, there's a workable path.
        </p>

        <p>
          If you're in the middle of planning a move in the Denver metro area—in <a href="/cities/boulder">Boulder</a>, <a href="/cities/golden">Golden</a>, <a href="/cities/littleton">Littleton</a>, <a href="/cities/arvada">Arvada</a>, or anywhere else across the Front Range—and storage is part of the picture, reach out early. The more lead time we have, the better we can set things up so the gap in your timeline doesn't turn into a headache.
        </p>

        <p>
          You can see more about how we handle storage as part of a full-service move on our <a href="/services/storage-solutions">storage solutions page</a>, or visit our <a href="/storage-services">storage services page</a> for a broader overview. And if you're looking to move quickly, our <a href="/same-day-moves">same-day moves</a> page covers what we can do when timing is tight.
        </p>

        <div class="bg-blue-50 border-l-4 border-blue-500 p-6 my-8">
          <p class="font-semibold text-secondary mb-2">Have a Gap in Your Move Timeline? We Can Help.</p>
          <p class="text-muted-foreground mb-4">
            Whether you need overnight truck storage or a longer-term storage solution, our team can coordinate the right option for your situation. Get in touch and let's figure out a plan.
          </p>
          <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-3">
            <a href="/get-quote" class={buttonVariants({ className: "bg-primary text-white hover:bg-secondary" })}>
              Get a Free Quote
            </a>
            <a href="tel:7203401849" class={buttonVariants({ variant: 'outline', className: "border-primary text-primary hover:bg-primary hover:text-white" })}>
              Call (720) 340-1849
            </a>
          </div>
        </div>

      </div>
    </div>
  </article>

